-- title:
--   Linear part of a Γ₀(M')-relabelling on Drinfeld origin parameters
-- statement:
--   The setting is fixed by the following data. A prime $q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$, and a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$ (hypotheses `hℓg`, `hℓg12`, `hℓgM'`); a base commutative ring $A_0$ in which $\ell_g$ is invertible (`hℓA`). Three transport hypotheses record that the level structures are stable under Weierstrass variable changes over arbitrary $A_0$-algebras: `hℓ` carries [`ModularCurve.IsGamma1Point`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma1Pow.html#L10) for $\ell_g$ along `LevelPData.variableChange`, `hM` carries [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) along [`ModularCurve.kernelVariableChangeDeg`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassLevelComponents.html#L104) with the degree `gamma0PowDeg`, and `hL` carries divisibility of [`ModularCurve.inLineMulPoly`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassH1Pow.html#L18) along the same operation on kernel polynomials. A family of relative group laws $\mathcal G$ on the projective models of Weierstrass curves with unit discriminant is assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`, each law admits a points evaluation) and to have the origin as identity (`h𝒢O`, each law admits an origin chart section of its unit with vanishing $x/y$ and $z/y$), and a level transport $\mathcal T$ for $\mathcal G$ at $q$ is assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`, its variable-change and coefficient-change operations are compatible with the two marked sections through the graded maps on projective models). Two further hypotheses provide the graded ring homomorphisms used for that comparison: `hVC` gives, for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and variable change $C$, a graded homomorphism `projModelGradingCR W →+*ᵍ projModelGradingCR (C • W)` pulling back the irrelevant ideal and satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom`, and `hCO` gives the analogous homomorphism for base change along an $A_0$-algebra map, satisfying `IsCoefficientHom`.
--
--   The moduli problem is `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯`: its raw points over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ consist of a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant together with a triple (a family of cyclic-kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a `LevelPData` which is a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point, and a Drinfeld pair of sections of level $q$ for $\mathcal G$), subject in addition to the linking divisibility `IsGamma1Link`. Here $P_0$ is a fine moduli package `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for the associated level moduli datum, that is, an $A_0$-algebra $P_0.B_0$ carrying a point $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ which represents the functor of points, and $x$ is a raw point over $P_0.B_0$ whose class is $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ (`hx`).
--
--   The deformation ring is a noetherian local domain $R$, complete for its maximal ideal and an $A_0$-algebra, equipped with an $A_0$-algebra map $\iota : P_0.B_0 \to R$. A field $k$ of characteristic $q$ in which $\ell_g$ and $M'$ are nonzero (`hℓk`, `hM'k`) is given with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal of $R$ (`hresR`, `hkerR`). A complete discrete valuation domain $W_0$ with maximal ideal generated by $q$ (`hW₀`) is given with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_0 : W_0 \to k$ of kernel the maximal ideal (`hres₀`, `hker₀`), together with algebra structures making $A_0 \to W_0 \to R$ a scalar tower and $\mathrm{res}_R$ compatible with $\mathrm{res}_0$ (`hresR₀`). The hypothesis `hfac` is a factorisation property: for every Artinian local $W_0$- and $A_0$-algebra $T$ in the same tower, with a surjection $\mathrm{res}_T : T \to k$ of kernel the maximal ideal and compatible with $\mathrm{res}_0$, every $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : P_0.B_0 \to T$ that lifts $\mathrm{res}_R \circ \iota$ factors through a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \Phi = \mathrm{res}_R$ and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   On the formal side, $F$ is a commutative formal group over $R$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of the curve of the Drinfeld-pair component of the base change of $x$ along $\iota$ (`hFW`), and $F_0$ is a commutative formal group over $k$ whose power series is that of the reduction of the same curve along $\mathrm{res}_R$ (`hF₀W`), subject to `hF₀`: with respect to the zero ideal of $k$, the $q$-th series of $F_0$ is a unit multiple of its Drinfeld divisor at $(0,0)$. The hypothesis `hssJ` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring map $\iota_\Omega : k \to \Omega$, the image of the residue $\mathrm{res}_R(\iota(P_0.j_0))$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), that is, every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. Two ring homomorphisms $\chi_P, \chi_Q$ from the origin chart ring of that curve to $R$ are given such that the sections $P$ and $Q$ of the Drinfeld pair reduce to the origin in the sense of `ReducesToOrigin` (`hP`, `hQ`): each $\chi$ is an origin chart section for the corresponding section, and both `originParam χ` and `originW χ` lie in the maximal ideal of $R$. Writing $x_0 =$ `originParam χP` and $x_1 =$ `originParam χQ`, the remaining hypotheses are: $F_0$ is the base change of $F$ along $\mathrm{res}_R$ (`hBC`); $(x_0,x_1)$ is an adic Drinfeld basis of level $q$ for $F$ relative to the maximal ideal (`hDr`, i.e. the $q$-th series of $F$ is a unit multiple of the Drinfeld divisor at $(x_0,x_1)$); the maximal ideal of $R$ is generated by $x_0$ and $x_1$ (`hmax`); and `huniv`, the universal property of $(R, F, x_0, x_1)$ among Artinian local $W_0$-algebras $T$ with a residue map to $k$ as above: for every commutative formal group $G$ over $T$ that is the base change of $F_0$ along $\mathrm{res}_T$, and all $y_0, y_1$ in the maximal ideal of $T$ forming an adic Drinfeld basis of level $q$ for $G$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\varphi : R \to T$ with $\mathrm{res}_T \circ \varphi = \mathrm{res}_R$ for which there exist a formal group $F'$ over $T$ that is the base change of $F$ along $\varphi$ and an isomorphism of laws $\psi : F' \to G$ whose series has coefficients reducing modulo the maximal ideal of $T$ to those of the identity series, and whose adic application sends $\varphi(x_0)$ to $y_0$ and $\varphi(x_1)$ to $y_1$.
--
--   Finally, $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ lies in $\Gamma_0(M')$ (`hγ`), and $\rho_\gamma$ is an automorphism of the moduli problem (a family of bijections on points, natural in the algebra and preserving the $j$-invariant) which is pinned to relabelling by $\gamma$ on field points by `hpin`: for every field $T$ that is an $A_0$-algebra and all raw points $y, y'$ over $T$ such that the discriminant of the curve of $y$'s Drinfeld pair is a unit, if $y'$ and $y$ have the same curve and the same kernel polynomials, if the affine point attached to the $\Gamma_1$-coordinates of $y'$ equals $\gamma_{00}$ times the affine point attached to those of $y$ on the base-changed curve, if the $Q$-coordinates of $y'$ coincide with its $P$-coordinates, and if the Drinfeld pair of $y'$ is `RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢 γ` applied to that of $y$ (the pair $(\gamma_{00}P + \gamma_{10}Q,\ \gamma_{01}P + \gamma_{11}Q)$ formed in the group law), then $\rho_\gamma$ carries the class of $y$ to the class of $y'$. The induced endomorphism of $P_0.B_0$, namely $P_0$-classification of $\rho_\gamma(P_0.\mathrm{univ})$, fixes the closed point: $\iota(P_0.\mathrm{classify}(\rho_\gamma(P_0.\mathrm{univ}))(b)) - \iota(b)$ lies in the maximal ideal for all $b$ (`hfix`). Lastly $\theta_0 : R \to R$ is a $W_0$-algebra endomorphism compatible with that endomorphism through $\iota$ (`hcompl`) and congruent to the identity modulo the maximal ideal (`hres`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exists $c \in R$ such that, with $\mathfrak m$ the maximal ideal of $R$:
--   $$\theta_0(x_0) - c\,(\gamma_{00}x_0 + \gamma_{10}x_1) \in \mathfrak m^2, \qquad \theta_0(x_1) - c\,(\gamma_{01}x_0 + \gamma_{11}x_1) \in \mathfrak m^2;$$
--   $c^{q+1} - 1 \in \mathfrak m$; if $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell_g}$ then $c - 1 \in \mathfrak m$; if $\gamma_{11} \equiv -1 \pmod{\ell_g}$ then $c + 1 \in \mathfrak m$; and if $\gamma$ lies in the principal congruence subgroup $\Gamma(q)$, $\gamma_{11} \not\equiv 1 \pmod{\ell_g}$, and in the case $q = 2$ also $\gamma_{11} \not\equiv -1 \pmod{\ell_g}$, then $c - 1 \notin \mathfrak m$.
--
--   This is the computation of the linear part, on the Drinfeld origin parameters $x_0, x_1$, of the endomorphism of the rigidified universal deformation ring of a supersingular point induced by a relabelling automorphism of the moduli problem with $\Gamma_0(M')$-, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$- and full level-$q$ Drinfeld structure: it acts by $\gamma$ up to a scalar $c$ with $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, and the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ guard pins $c$ to $\pm 1$ or keeps it away from $1$. It feeds the construction of the ring isomorphism realising such an automorphism on the deformation ring.
